    "category": "Living",
    "title": "Check the Center: A Two-Person Food-Thermometer Habit",
    "subtitle": "Daily Read — Make the thermometer a shared reference point, not one person’s job to supervise.",
    "imageAlt": "Two friends cook together at a home kitchen counter; one checks a pan of chicken with a food thermometer while the other consults a temperature chart on a phone.",
    "imageConcept": "A warmly lit, lived-in kitchen with two friends at the stove: one holding a thermometer in a piece of chicken and the other reading a simple temperature chart on a phone, with serving plates and ordinary cooking tools nearby.",
    "body": [
      [
        "Start with the tool, not a guess",
        "A thermometer is a small piece of kitchen equipment with an outsized job: it tells you whether the inside of food has reached a safe temperature. That is different from checking whether the surface looks browned or the juices seem clear. Correct thermometer placement helps ensure an accurate internal-temperature reading, so a reading from the right spot gives you a more dependable basis for deciding when food is ready [1]. The USDA Food Safety and Inspection Service says correct thermometer placement is important for an accurate internal-temperature reading [1].\n\nBefore cooking, find the thermometer, check its instructions and make sure you know how its display works. Thermometers can differ in how much of the stem is the sensing area [3]. If the tool has been misplaced in a drawer or shared between households, take a moment to inspect it and follow its care instructions. Getting familiar with it before the pan is hot makes the check less hurried."
      ],
      [
        "Make it a two-person task",
        "Cooking together doesn’t have to mean one person directs while the other follows. Try a simple division of attention: one person handles the thermometer, while the other checks the recommended temperature for the food being cooked. The person reading can identify the target aloud; the person probing can say when the display settles. Then swap roles the next time. It’s a small way to make the check collaborative rather than a test of anyone’s judgment.\n\nThis works whether you’re cooking with a partner, a friend, a relative or a housemate. It can also help when one person is more comfortable with kitchen equipment and another is more comfortable finding information. Neither role is the “expert” role; both contribute to a shared decision. If you’re cooking alone, the same sequence still works: find the target first, then take the reading, rather than trying to remember a number in the middle of dinner."
      ],
      [
        "Put the probe in the right place",
        "Where you measure matters. For a thick piece of meat, insert the thermometer into the thickest part, avoiding bone, fat and gristle. For a thin food, such as a cutlet or a thin patty, insert the probe from the side so its sensing area reaches the center. USDA guidance describes these placement differences because a reading from the wrong spot may not represent the food’s interior [1].\n\nA practical approach: have the person probing point out the thickest section before inserting the thermometer. The other person can look for bone or a particularly thin edge and help identify a better route. Keep the probe away from the pan when taking the reading; the goal is the food’s internal temperature, not the heat of the cooking surface. Follow the thermometer’s instructions for how deep to insert it and how long to wait. If the food is uneven, check more than one area."
      ],
      [
        "Use a chart instead of memory",
        "Temperature targets vary by food, so keep an authoritative chart somewhere easy to reach—saved on a phone, bookmarked on a computer or printed and tucked into a cupboard. FoodSafety.gov’s chart lists 165°F for poultry, 160°F for ground meats, and 145°F for fish and for many whole cuts of beef, pork, lamb and veal, followed by a three-minute rest for those whole cuts [2]. Check the chart for the specific food you’re making rather than assuming one number applies to everything.\n\nThe reader can say the food category and target; the person holding the thermometer can repeat them back. That brief exchange catches a common source of confusion: a target remembered from last night’s meal may not fit tonight’s. For a dish with several components, use the chart that matches the item you’re measuring. The temperature is a reference for the inside of the food, while any recommended rest time is a separate step after it reaches the target."
      ],
      [
        "Check each piece, not just the first",
        "A single reading can’t stand in for every piece in a crowded pan. Size, thickness and position can differ, and a large or irregular item may not heat evenly. Check each piece of poultry, burger or cutlet rather than assuming the one nearest the center represents the rest. With a large roast or another uneven shape, take readings in more than one spot, using the thermometer’s instructions to choose appropriate insertion points.\n\nWorking in pairs makes the sequence easier to track without turning dinner into an inspection. One person can move pieces to a clean plate after they’re checked; the other can take the readings and call out which ones still need attention. If a piece is below its target, return it to the heat and check it again. Keep raw food and ready-to-eat food separate, and don’t place cooked food back on a plate that held raw meat unless it has been washed."
      ],
      [
        "Keep the routine clean and calm",
        "A thermometer is a food-contact tool, so clean its stem according to its care instructions. Wash it before use and between checks when it could transfer raw juices to cooked food. Don’t immerse or rinse a device in a way its manufacturer advises against; the instructions for your specific model take priority.\n\nIf a reading seems surprising, don’t argue from appearances. Check that the probe is inserted correctly, wait for the display to settle, and take another reading in a suitable spot. If you’re unsure whether the thermometer is accurate, consult its instructions for checking or calibration. A calm second check is more useful than pressing harder, guessing from the color or letting one person’s confidence substitute for a measurement."
      ],
      [
        "Make the chart part of the kitchen",
        "A good shared habit is one you can repeat without a meeting. Choose a place for the thermometer and the chart that makes sense in your kitchen: a drawer near the stove, a note on the fridge or a saved link on a phone both cooks can reach. Before starting, agree on who will probe and who will read the target. After the first reading, switch roles if that feels natural; there’s no need to make every dinner a lesson.\n\nIf cooking with children or someone new to the kitchen, keep responsibilities appropriate to their age and experience. They might help locate the chart or call out the target while an adult handles the hot pan and probe. The point is not to make the process formal. It is to replace a vague question—“Does this look done?”—with a clear one: “What does the thermometer say, and is that the right target for this food?”"
      ],
      [
        "Finish with a shared check, not a verdict",
        "At your next meal cooked with someone else, try the routine on just one item. Before the food goes on the heat, find the thermometer and the chart together. Agree on the target and decide who will probe first; let the other person read. Check the right place, then check every piece that needs its own reading. If anything falls short, return it to the heat and repeat the measurement. Clean the tool as its instructions direct, and put it back where you both can find it. The result isn’t a score for either cook. It’s a practical piece of information you gathered together—and a kitchen habit you can adjust to suit the people sharing the meal."
      ]
    ],
