    "category": "Living",
    "title": "The Room-by-Room Record: Build a Home Inventory Together",
    "subtitle": "Daily Read — A shared record of what’s at home can make a difficult claim process easier to document. Start with one room, keep the details factual, and store the record somewhere you can reach if the house is not.",
    "imageAlt": "Three family members sit together in a living room, photographing a lamp and writing down its details.",
    "imageConcept": "three generations of one family working together together, in a specific visual scene about “The Room-by-Room Record: Build a Home Inventory Together”: A warm, lived-in living room where three generations collaborate around a notebook and phone: one person names an object, another photographs it, and a third checks the written record. Roles feel shared and interchangeable; the room, not age, is the focus.",
    "body": [
      [
        "A record, not a renovation project",
        "A home inventory can sound like a task for a rainy weekend and an empty house. It need not be. Think of it as a useful record of the belongings in your home: what an item is, where it is, and, when relevant, identifying details such as its model or serial number. Photographs or video can add context that a list alone may miss. Insurance regulators recommend documenting belongings before a loss and keeping the record somewhere accessible away from the home.[2]\n\nThe point is not to create a perfect catalogue or to prove that every possession is covered. An inventory may help you describe what was in the home when you file a claim, but it does not determine coverage, limits, deductibles, or claim requirements. Those depend on your policy and applicable rules. Treat the record as one practical piece of household preparation—not a promise about what an insurer will pay."
      ],
      [
        "Make the first unit one room",
        "Starting with the whole home is a reliable way to make the task feel bigger than it is. Choose one room instead: the bedroom, kitchen, or the corner where electronics and hobby equipment tend to gather. A room-sized first pass is an editorial approach to making the work manageable, not a tested time-saving formula. The goal is simply to make a clear beginning and a record you can continue later.\n\nBefore documenting, decide what level of detail is useful. A broad room photo gives an overview; closer images can show individual belongings and identifying marks. For items whose make or model could matter, include the label or note the information separately. You do not have to record every low-value object with equal precision. A clear description, a photograph, and useful identifying details for selected items are more helpful than an elaborate system no one can maintain. The National Association of Insurance Commissioners recommends going room by room and using photographs or video to document items.[1]"
      ],
      [
        "Let the work move between people",
        "A shared inventory works best as a small collaboration, not a family assignment handed to one person. One person can name an item while another takes a photograph; someone else can note the description or check a model number. Then rotate. The person who knows how a piece of equipment is used may not be the person most comfortable with a phone camera, and neither skill needs to belong to a particular generation.\n\nKeep the record itself factual. Write “oak dining table, six chairs” rather than trying to capture the table’s whole family history in the inventory. But a brief separate note—a gift, a maker’s mark, the name of a craftsperson—can preserve context that matters to the household. That distinction makes room for memory without confusing sentimental value with an insurance valuation. For a shared home, agree on who can view or update the record and where the current version lives. A useful record is one household members can find and understand."
      ],
      [
        "Photograph for context, then add detail",
        "Begin with a slow sweep of the room, taking in the walls, furniture, and visible belongings. If video is easier, narrate a plain description as you move: what the item is and where it sits. Then photograph selected items more closely, especially those with useful identifying details. California’s Department of Insurance guide recommends photos or video and notes that an inventory can include descriptions, values, and serial numbers.[2]\n\nFor a specific object, a simple entry might read: “Laptop, silver, brand and model from underside label, used at desk.” If the label is hard to read, take a close-up rather than relying on memory. Keep purchase receipts, appraisals, or repair records where relevant, but do not make collecting paperwork a prerequisite for documenting the room. If you are unsure whether an item needs a separate note, capture enough information that someone who does not know your home could recognize it later. Avoid turning a quick record into a search for perfect proof."
      ],
      [
        "Store it where a loss cannot take it",
        "A record saved only on a device that stays at home may be difficult to reach after a disaster. Save a copy in a secure location accessible away from the property—for example, a protected online account or another secure off-site option. The Federal Trade Commission’s guidance on organizing important papers also recommends keeping important records safely accessible and reviewing household documents at least annually.[3] Choose storage that fits your privacy needs and the devices or accounts you already use.\n\nThink through access as well as security. Could an authorized household member find the record if you were unavailable? Can you sign in from another device? Keep passwords and recovery methods protected, and avoid sending sensitive files broadly just because several people helped create them. A second copy can be useful, but it should also be stored thoughtfully. The practical test is straightforward: if you had to leave home, could you reach the inventory and understand what it contains without returning for the original?"
      ],
      [
        "Keep it current without chasing perfection",
        "An inventory loses usefulness when it becomes a one-time snapshot that no longer resembles the home. The NAIC advises reviewing it annually and updating it after purchases; it also recommends retaining receipts for new items and repairs when filing a homeowners claim.[1] You can make maintenance modest: add a new significant purchase when it arrives, remove or mark items that have left, and check the record once a year for obvious gaps.\n\nDo not assume the same level of documentation is appropriate for every possession. A receipt may help establish details about a recent purchase; a photograph may help identify a distinctive item; a serial number may help distinguish one model from another. What matters depends on the item and your circumstances. If you are using the inventory to prepare for a potential claim, read your policy and ask your insurer or agent about documentation expectations. A household record can support the process, but it cannot replace policy language or professional guidance about coverage."
      ],
      [
        "Give one room its first clear record",
        "For your first pass, choose a room that feels approachable and set out a phone or camera, a notebook, and somewhere secure to save the finished files. Take an overview photo or a slow video, then choose a few belongings to photograph more closely. Write down descriptions and identifying details where useful; add a receipt or related document if it is already easy to locate. Ask everyone helping to take a turn, and keep personal memories in a separate note from the factual inventory.\n\nBefore you stop, check that the images and notes are saved somewhere you can reach away from home. Write the room name and date on the record, then decide who will make the next update and when you will review it. The aim is not to finish the house in one sitting. It is to leave one room documented clearly enough that the next room has a simple, familiar model to follow."
      ]
    ],
